Hey all! Being able to propagate client certificate information as event metadata is a _really_ useful feature that I've used a lot with logstash in the past. From what I can tell, there isn't a capability to pass along that metadata with vector, at least with the socket and http input plugins that I could find.

It's a helpful feature for being able to collect metrics on specific users/applications while also providing some rudimentary ACLs for outputs when used in conjunction with the [translate](https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/logstash/current/plugins-filters-translate.html) filter, not to mention the added security benefit of using TLS.

Here's an example pipeline I've used before to map a beats input source to a given kafka topic based on the certificate principal they present(great for handling many distinct users over a single port):
```perl
input {
  beats {
    port => 7000
    ssl => true
    ssl_verify_mode => "force_peer"
    ssl_certificate_authorities => [
      "/etc/ssl/beats_server/ca.pem"
    ]
    ssl_certificate => "/etc/ssl/beats_server/certificate.pem"
    ssl_key => "/etc/ssl/beats_server/certificate-key_pkcs8.pem"
    ssl_peer_metadata => true
  }
}
filter {
  translate {
    field => "[@metadata][tls_peer][subject]"
    destination => "[@metadata][destination_topic]"
    dictionary_path => "/etc/logstash/cert_to_topic.yml"
    fallback => "catchall.topic"
    exact => true
  }
}

output {
  kafka {
    ...
    topic_id => "%{[@metadata][destination_topic]}"
  }
}
```

Where the dictionary path in the filter is a file That consists of a mapping of cert principal strings and topic names.

Not really sure how vector handles internal event metadata, but I was curious if you all would be open to supporting client certificate principal data to be extracted from incoming TLS connections(probably with the `socket` and `http` sources at first, but if there's interest maybe this feature could trickle out into some other sources, like the HEC input?) and maybe a translate-like transform(though I'm happy to open another issue for tracking the discussion on that.

What are your thoughts?

